but Hittmeir squeezes much more out of it. Among other things, he s hows that the
relation pq = N forces certain restrictions on the residue of p + q modulo various
small primes, which can be used to speed up the BSGS search.
More recently, in a spectacular preprint [Hit20], Hittmeir presented an algorithm
achieving
F(N ) = O(N 2/9 log11/3 N ).
Notice that 2 /9 = 0 .222 . . . < 0.25 = 1 /4. This result represents the ﬁrst exponen-
tial speedup for the problem of integer factorisation since the 197 0s. The algorithm
has many features in common with [Hit18], but instead of searching f or p + q, it
searches for the linear combinations aq + bp that arise in Lehman’s algorithm. The
replacement for (1.3) in this context is the congruence
αaq+bp ≡ αaN +b (mod p),
which follows from Fermat’s little theorem. His algorithm actually examin es many
more candidates than Lehman’s algorithm, but the complexity is dras tically reduced
thanks to the use of the BSGS search.
The algorithm described in the present paper achieves a further ex ponential
speedup, from N 2/9+o(1) to N 1/5+o(1) = N 0.2+o(1). It is based largely on Hittmeir’s
algorithm from [Hit20]. The main diﬀerence, and the source of the exp onential
speedup, is that we apply the BSGS search technique much more agg ressively.
Whereas Hittmeir breaks up the search space into chunks and applie s the BSGS
search to each chunk separately, we show how to sweep through t he entire space
with a single BSGS search. For a more quantitative discussion of the r elationship
between the two algorithms, see Remark 3.4.
We also make one other small improvement to Hittmeir’s algorithm: we r eplace
a certain multipoint evaluation step with an invocation of Bluestein’s alg orithm
[Blu70] (see Algorithm 4.1). This saves a further factor of (log N )O(1) overall.
2. Preliminaries
In this section we introduce various notation and terminology, and r ecall several
ingredients from the literature that will be needed later. All results stated in this
section without speciﬁc references may be found in standard text books on computer
algebra, such as [vzGG13] or [BZ11].
For n a positive integer, we deﬁne lg n := ⌈log n/ log 2⌉ if n ⩾ 2, and lg 1 := 1.
Clearly lg n = Θ(log n) as n → ∞ .
Given a list of n ⩾ 1 elements of bit size β ⩾ 1, we may sort the list using
the “merge sort” algorithm in time O(nβ lg n) [Knu98]. Here we assume that the
elements belong to some totally ordered set, and that we may compa re a pair of
elements in time O(β).
2.1. Integer arithmetic. Let n ⩾ 1, and assume that we are given integers x and
y such that |x|, |y|⩽ 2n.
• We may compute x + y and x − y in time O(n).
• We write M(n) for the cost of computing the product xy. As mentioned in
the Introduction, we may take M(n) = O(n lg n) [HvdH19].
• If y > 0, we may compute the quotients ⌊x/y⌋ and ⌈x/y⌉ in time O(M(n)).
We may therefore also compute the residue of x modulo y in the interval
[0, y) in time O(M(n)).

