• More generally, for a ﬁxed positive rational number u/v, and assuming that
x, y > 0, we may compute ⌊(x/y)u/v⌋ and ⌈(x/y)u/v⌉ in time O(M(n)).
• Using the “half-GCD” algorithm, we may compute g := gcd( x, y), and if
desired ﬁnd integers u and v such that ux + vy = g, in time O(M(n) lg n) =
O(n lg2 n). In particular, if gcd( x, y) = 1, we may compute the inverse of
x modulo y in time O(M(n) lg n).
Let N ⩾ 2. We write ZN for the ring Z/NZ of integers modulo N . Elements of
ZN will always be represented by their residues in the interval [0 , N). In particular,
an element of ZN occupies at most lg N bits of storage. We write Z∗
N for the group
of units of ZN , i.e., the subset of those x ∈ ZN for which gcd( x, N) = 1.
• Given x, y ∈ ZN , we may compute x ± y ∈ ZN in time O(lg N ), and we
may compute xy ∈ ZN in time O(M(lg N )) = O(lg N lg lg N ).
• Given x ∈ ZN and an integer m ⩾ 0, we may compute xm ∈ ZN in time
O(M(lg N ) lg m), using the “repeated squaring” algorithm.
• Given x ∈ ZN , we may test whether x ∈ Z∗
N in time O(M(lg N ) lg lg N ),
by computing gcd( x, N). If this GCD is not 1 or N , and if N = pq is a
semiprime, then we immediately recover p and q in time O(M(lg N )).
2.2. Polynomial arithmetic. We always represent a polynomial in ZN [x] by a
list of its coeﬃcients. For d ⩾ 1, let MN (d) denote the cost of multiplying two
polynomials in ZN [x] of degree at most d.
Lemma 2.1. If d = O(N ) then
MN (d) = O(d lg2 N ). (2.1)
Proof. Using Kronecker substitution, i.e., packing the coeﬃcients into large integers
and then multiplying these integers (see [vzGG13, Cor. 8.27]), we ob tain
MN (d) = O(M(d(lg N + lg d))).
Under the hypothesis d = O(N ) this simpliﬁes to O(d lg2 N ). □
Remark 2.2. In the above lemma, the hypothesis “ d = O(N )” means that for every
constant C > 0, the lemma holds for all d and N in the region d < CN , where
the implied big- O constant in (2.1) may depend on C. For the rest of the paper, a
similar remark applies whenever we use the big- O notation in this way.
Lemma 2.3. Let n ⩾ 1 with n = O(N ). Given as input v1, . . . , vn ∈ ZN , we may
compute the polynomial f (x) = ( x − v1) · · ·(x − vn) ∈ ZN [x] in time
O(n lg3 N ).
Proof. This is a standard application of a product tree: starting with the line ar
polynomials x − vi, we repeatedly multiply pairs of polynomials together, with the
degree doubling at each level. There are O(lg n) = O(lg N ) levels, and Lemma 2.1
implies that the cost at each level is O(n lg2 N ). For further details, see for instance
[vzGG13, Algorithm 10.3]. □
