ﬁnd elements of large order in Z∗
N , is due to Hittmeir. Of course in practice this is
easy, because almost every element of Z∗
N has large order. The point of Hittmeir’s
result is that the algorithm is deterministic and gives provably correc t output.
Proposition 2.7 ([Hit18, Theorem 6.3]) . There is an algorithm with the following
properties. It takes as input integers N ⩾ 2 and D such that N 2/5 ⩽ D ⩽ N . It
returns either some α ∈ Z∗
N such that ordN (α) > D , or a nontrivial factor of N ,
or “ N is prime”. Its running time is
O(D1/2 lg2 N ).

