Our strategy (see Section 4) is to take r smaller again, namely r = N 1/5+o(1) =
N 0.2+o(1). The number of candidates increases even further to N 2/5+o(1) = N 0.4+o(1).
However, we perform a single BSGS search (similar to the method Hitt meir uses
for the large values of k) that covers all values of k simultaneously. In eﬀect, we
enjoy a “square-root speedup” over the entire search space.
An interesting question is whether it is possible to obtain a fully square -root
speedup for Lehman’s original choice r ≍ N 1/3. This would presumably lead to a
factoring algorithm with complexity N 1/6+o(1).
4. The main algorithm
Before discussing the main search algorithm, we ﬁrst consider Algor ithm 4.1,
which ﬁnds collisions modulo p or q between two subsets of ZN . This subroutine is
almost identical to Algorithm 3.2 of [Hit20]. However, whereas Hittme ir allows both
subsets to be essentially arbitrary, we specialise to the case that o ne of the subsets
consists entirely of powers of some ﬁxed α ∈ Z∗
N . This allows us to improve the
complexity from O((n + m) lg3 N ) (see [Hit20, Lem. 3.3]) to O(n lg3 N + m lg2 N ).
Algorithm 4.1 – ﬁnding collisions modulo p or q
Input:
– A positive integer N , either prime or semiprime.
– A positive integer m, and an element α ∈ Z∗
N with ord N (α) ⩾ m.
– Elements v1, . . . , vn ∈ ZN for some positive integer n, such that vh ̸= αi for
all h ∈ { 1, . . . , n} and i ∈ { 0, . . . , m− 1}.
Output:
– If N = pq is a semiprime, and if there exists h ∈ { 1, . . . , n} such that vh ≡ αi
(mod p) or vh ≡ αi (mod q) for some i ∈ { 0, . . . , m− 1}, returns p and q.
– Otherwise returns “no factors found”.
1) Using Lemma 2.3 (product tree), compute the polynomial
f (x) := (x − v1) · · ·(x − vn) ∈ ZN [x].
2) Using Lemma 2.4 (Bluestein’s algorithm), compute the values f (αi) ∈ ZN for
i = 0, . . . , m− 1.
3) For i = 0, . . . , m− 1:
a) Compute γi := gcd(N, f (αi)).
b) If γi /∈ { 1, N}, recover p and q and return.
c) If γi = N , then for h = 1, . . . , n:
i) If gcd( N, vh − αi) ̸= 1, recover p and q and return.
4) Return “no factors found”.
Proposition 4.1. Algorithm 4.1 is correct. For m, n = O(N ), its running time is
O(n lg3 N + m lg2 N ).
Proof. We ﬁrst prove correctness. Suppose that N = pq is a semiprime, and that
vh0 ≡ αi0 modulo p or modulo q for some h0 and i0. Then f (αi0 ) includes the factor
vh0 − αi0 , so we have γi0 ̸= 1 in Step (3a). If additionally γi0 ̸= N , then p and q
are successfully recovered in Step (3b). Suppose however that γi0 = N . Then the

