desired ﬁnd u, v ∈ Z such that ux + vy = g (i.e., solve the extended GCD problem),
in time O(M(n) lg n) = O(n lg2 n).
Next we consider modular arithmetic. Let N ⩾ 2. We write ZN for the ring
Z/N Z of integers modulo N . Elements of ZN will always be represented by their
residues in the interval [0 , N ), so occupy O(lg N ) bits of storage. We write Z∗
N for
the group of units of ZN , i.e., the subset of those x ∈ ZN for which gcd( x, N ) = 1.
Given x, y ∈ ZN , we may compute x ± y ∈ ZN in time O(lg N ), and xy ∈ ZN
in time O(M(lg N )) = O(lg N lg lg N ). Given x ∈ ZN and an integer m ⩾ 1, we
may compute xm ∈ ZN in time O(M(lg N ) lg m), using the “repeated squaring”
algorithm. We may test whether x ∈ Z∗
N , and if so compute x− 1 ∈ Z∗
N , in time
O(M(lg N ) lg lg N ) = O(lg N (lg lg N )2), by solving the extended GCD problem for
x and N . If the gcd is not 1 or N , and if N = pq is semiprime, then we immediately
recover p and q in time O(M(lg N )).
